{"items":[{"targetId":"n0000126","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Equation 5 is typically the residual mixing update that left-multiplies stream states by a constrained mixing matrix H, so each layer’s streams are linearly recombined before the block function.","densityScore":0.62,"relation":"fills","reason":"Gives a usable placeholder for the missing residual-mix equation so later spectral claims have an object."}]},{"targetId":"n0000133","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Equation 6 is typically the manifold constraint or projection that forces H onto a stochastic/doubly-stochastic set (often via Sinkhorn or a geodesic step), rather than an unconstrained linear mix.","densityScore":0.64,"relation":"fills","reason":"Supplies the missing constraint equation that later method names (mHC, go-mHC) presuppose."}]},{"targetId":"n0000164","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"An AGM metric is the arithmetic–geometric mean of a positive spectrum (or of singular values): AGM(λ)=((1/n)∑λ_i) / (∏λ_i)^{1/n} or a logged variant, used to summarize spectral spread without a single extremal eigenvalue.","densityScore":0.7,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the unnamed comparison statistic so tables citing AGM are interpretable."},{"type":"uncertainty","text":"The paper’s exact AGM formula (which matrix, whether logs, whether restricted to mixing eigenvalues) is not recovered here.","densityScore":0.4,"relation":"supports","reason":"Prevents treating the placeholder formula as source-canonical."}]},{"targetId":"n0000165","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"P_go denotes the go-mHC mixing operator (or its projection onto the allowed manifold): the learned or optimized hyper-connection matrix used by the geometric method rather than a generic residual mix.","densityScore":0.66,"relation":"fills","reason":"Names the object later compared against P_M and unconstrained mixes."},{"type":"uncertainty","text":"Whether P_go is a projector, a probability, or the mixing matrix itself is not fixed without the source equation.","densityScore":0.35,"relation":"supports","reason":"Flags notation collision with projection symbols."}]},{"targetId":"n0000168","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Spectral reach is how far the mixing operator’s eigenvalues (or singular values) extend from 1: a larger reach means stronger amplification/attenuation across residual streams.","densityScore":0.68,"relation":"fills","reason":"Turns a cited diagnostic into a causal quantity linking constraint tightness to signal scale."}]},{"targetId":"n0000169","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"P_M is the (or a) projection onto the constraint manifold M of allowed mixing matrices, mapping an unconstrained H to a feasible neighbor used in the forward pass or in a retraction.","densityScore":0.72,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the geometric primitive that distinguishes manifold methods from unconstrained hyper-connections."}]},{"targetId":"n0000172","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Method M is a generic labeled baseline or ablated mixing scheme against which go-mHC is compared (unconstrained HC, mHC, or another projector), not a specific architecture name.","densityScore":0.5,"relation":"fills","reason":"Prevents reading M as a unique named model when it is a comparison slot."}]},{"targetId":"n0000207","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"An s-stochastic (row- or column-stochastic) matrix has nonnegative entries whose rows or columns sum to 1; doubly stochastic matrices are both, and live in the Birkhoff polytope.","densityScore":0.74,"relation":"fills","reason":"Gives the linear-algebra meaning behind constrained residual mixers."},{"type":"uncertainty","text":"The token “s-ssostochastic” is likely a parse of s-stochastic/doubly-stochastic; the exact paper coinage is unverified.","densityScore":0.38,"relation":"supports","reason":"Avoids inventing a new matrix class from a garbled label."}]},{"targetId":"n0000208","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Hyper-Connections (HC) mix several residual streams with a learned matrix; mHC constrains that matrix to a stochastic manifold (commonly doubly stochastic via Sinkhorn) so mixing stays norm-controlled.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the family the later variants modify."},{"type":"definition","text":"mHC-lite is a cheaper mHC: fewer Sinkhorn iterations, factored mixers, or a weaker (e.g. only row-stochastic) constraint to cut compute.","densityScore":0.6,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Separates the lite ablation from full manifold projection."},{"type":"definition","text":"KromHC uses a Kronecker (or other low-rank/factored) parameterization of the mixing matrix so the manifold constraint is applied in a smaller factor space.","densityScore":0.58,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the Krom- prefix as structure, not a different residual topology."},{"type":"definition","text":"go-mHC replaces a Euclidean/Sinkhorn-style step with geometric optimization on the mixing manifold (retraction, geodesic, or natural-gradient update of H).","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"fills","reason":"States what the paper’s method adds beyond mHC."}]},{"targetId":"n0000209","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"H_res is the residual-stream mixing matrix: it linearly combines the d parallel streams that wrap a transformer/block, analogous to a widened residual identity when H_res = I.","densityScore":0.73,"relation":"fills","reason":"Gives the symbol a role in the residual recurrence."}]},{"targetId":"n0000210","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"d-streams are d parallel residual feature streams; hyper-connections mix them each layer instead of using a single residual path.","densityScore":0.7,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the width axis that mixing matrices act on."}]},{"targetId":"n0000212","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A spectral hull is the convex hull (or a bounding region) of a mixing operator’s spectrum, used to summarize possible gain/phase of stream mixing rather than a single eigenvalue.","densityScore":0.63,"relation":"fills","reason":"Connects hull language to stability of residual mixing."},{"type":"uncertainty","text":"Whether the paper means numerical range, convex hull of eigenvalues, or a custom envelope is not recovered.","densityScore":0.36,"relation":"supports","reason":"Keeps the definition from over-claiming geometry."}]},{"targetId":"n0000213","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"i_k usually indexes layer, Sinkhorn iterate, or stream k; in mixing notation it often marks the k-th residual stream or the k-th projection step.","densityScore":0.45,"relation":"fills","reason":"Gives a default reading so later subscripts are not empty."},{"type":"uncertainty","text":"The paper’s specific i_k (identity block vs iterate vs stream id) needs the missing equation.","densityScore":0.32,"relation":"supports","reason":"Stops treating the index as a defined tensor."}]}]}
